      title: "Sahagún and the Florentine Codex",
      body: `## A friar in New Spain

Much of what we know about Aztec culture comes from a single extraordinary work: the ***General History of the Things of New Spain***, known as the ***Florentine Codex***, compiled under the direction of the Franciscan friar **Bernardino de Sahagún** (c. 1499–1590). Sahagún was born in Spain, studied at the University of Salamanca, and arrived in Mexico in **1529**, eight years after the conquest, as one of the early Franciscan missionaries. He spent the rest of his long life in Mexico, more than sixty years, and became one of the most remarkable scholars of the sixteenth century.

## The Colegio de Santa Cruz

In **1536**, the Franciscans founded the **Colegio de Santa Cruz de Tlatelolco**, a school for the sons of the Aztec nobility, where they were taught Latin, rhetoric, philosophy, music, and medicine, as well as Christian doctrine. The school produced a generation of brilliant Native scholars, fluent in Nahuatl, Spanish, and Latin, who became Sahagún's collaborators. Among them were **Antonio Valeriano**, who later became governor of Tenochtitlan, **Martín Jacobita**, **Alonso Vegerano**, and **Pedro de San Buenaventura**.

## The method

Sahagún's purpose was missionary: he believed that to convert the Aztecs effectively and root out their "idolatry," the friars needed to understand their religion, customs, and language. "The physician cannot advisedly administer medicines to the patient," he wrote, "without first knowing of which humor or from which source the ailment derives." Beginning in the 1540s and 1550s, he and his Native collaborators interviewed elders who remembered the world before the conquest, in the towns of **Tepepulco**, **Tlatelolco**, and Tenochtitlan. The elders answered questions and, in many cases, provided painted documents in the traditional manner, which the Native scholars explained in Nahuatl. The material was organized, revised, and expanded over some thirty years.

## The work

The result, completed around **1577–1579**, was a work in **twelve books**, written in two parallel columns: the Nahuatl text on the right, and a Spanish translation and paraphrase, often abbreviated, on the left. It was illustrated with some **2,400** paintings by Native artists, combining European and Mesoamerican styles. The twelve books covered the gods; the ceremonies and calendar; the origins of the gods; astrology and divination; omens; rhetoric and moral philosophy (including the *huehuetlatolli*); the sun, moon, and stars; the rulers; the merchants; the people, their virtues and vices, their occupations, and their bodies and diseases; the natural world, its animals, plants, and minerals; and, in the twelfth book, the **conquest of Mexico**, told from the point of view of the people of Tlatelolco.

The *Florentine Codex* has been called the first work of modern anthropology. It is an encyclopedia of Aztec civilization, unparalleled in its scope and detail for any non-European culture of the time. Without it, our knowledge of Aztec religion, society, and thought would be immeasurably poorer.

## Suppression

Sahagún's work aroused suspicion. Some in the church and the Spanish government feared that studying the old religion might encourage its survival, and that writing in Nahuatl might delay the spread of Spanish. In **1577**, King **Philip II** ordered that Sahagún's manuscripts be confiscated and sent to Spain, and that no one be allowed to write about the "superstitions and ways of life" of the Indians. Sahagún complied, but he seems to have kept copies. The manuscript sent to Spain eventually reached the **Medici** library in **Florence**, perhaps as a gift to the grand duke, which is why it is called the *Florentine Codex*. It remains in the **Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana** in Florence. It was largely forgotten until the nineteenth century, and the first complete English translation of the Nahuatl text, by **Arthur J. O. Anderson** and **Charles E. Dibble**, was published in twelve volumes between 1950 and 1982. In 2023, the Getty Research Institute released a digital edition, making the entire manuscript available online.

## Whose voice?

Whose voice do we hear in the *Florentine Codex*? This question has occupied scholars for decades. The work was directed by a Spanish friar with missionary purposes, who chose the questions and organized the answers according to European categories, such as the encyclopedias of Pliny and medieval scholars. But the Nahuatl text was written by Native scholars, based on the testimony of Native elders, and it preserves their words, their categories, and their perspectives, sometimes in tension with Sahagún's own. The Spanish column often abbreviates, softens, or omits what the Nahuatl says. Scholars today emphasize the role of the Native collaborators as co-authors, not merely informants. The work is a product of the encounter between two worlds, shaped by both.

## Book Twelve

The twelfth book, on the conquest, is particularly significant. It tells the story of the Spanish invasion from the point of view of the Mexica of Tlatelolco, based on the memories of survivors. It describes the omens, Moctezuma's anxiety, the arrival of the Spaniards, the massacre of nobles at the festival of Toxcatl, the expulsion of the Spaniards, the smallpox epidemic, the siege, and the final surrender. It was the basis of Miguel León-Portilla's collection *The Broken Spears* (1959), which brought the Native view of the conquest to a wide audience. Its account of the conquest, written decades afterward, must be read critically—it includes, for example, the story of Moctezuma's belief that Cortés was Quetzalcoatl, which we discussed in the previous lesson. But it is an irreplaceable record of how the conquered remembered their defeat.

## The legacy

Sahagún's work stands at the beginning of a long tradition of scholarship on the Aztecs, and of Nahuatl literacy. In the colonial period, Nahua scribes produced thousands of documents in their own language—wills, land records, petitions, annals, and histories—which historians such as **James Lockhart**, in *The Nahuas after the Conquest* (1992), have used to reconstruct the history of the Nahua people under Spanish rule from their own point of view. Nahuatl is still spoken today by some 1.7 million people in Mexico.

> **Pause and consider.** The *Florentine Codex* was directed by a Spanish missionary but written by Native scholars from the testimony of Native elders. Whose voice do we hear in it, and how can we tell?`,
